The ground here is clean flat grass — a good, quiet site. I've surveyed enough. Let me record my plan and build.

Design: **The Hollow Spire** — a tapering open lattice of stone (echoing the beacons around here, but hollow where they're solid), with a glass heart that glows at night, set on a low stone podium. It answers the neighbours' verticals with a different massing: light passing through, not around.

{"action":"note","text":"Site: ~x1140,z1150, flat grass, open to SE of the river & the Commons cluster. Plan: Hollow Spire — 7×7 stone podium (y1-2), tapering hollow stone ring lattice y3-14 (outer square 7→3), glass core column, gold+light heart at top, moss tufts on podium. Style: 'Lattice Luminism'. Build with parametric box/walls ops in batches <2048 blocks, protect_existing:true."}

I see two issues: my glass heart column is too tall and reads as a separate floating slab (it pokes well above the spire and sits off-center), and the spire itself looks a bit squat/lumpy from this angle. Let me remove the over-tall glass column and rebuild a tighter, properly-centered core that tucks *into* the hollow spire rather than towering over it.

First, remove the stray tall glass column.

